{Notes from Junior Chess Magazine.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5.
f3 {The Saemisch Variation. The f-pawn acts as a central buttress and as a
pivot for a pawn advance on the kingside.} 5... O-O 6. Be3 e5 7. d5 {Blockading
the centre ready for a pawn advance. Here, 7 Nge2 exd4 8 Nxd4 is also played.}
7... c6 8. Nge2 cxd5 9. cxd5 Ne8 {
Preparing for ...f7-f5 which, however, does not turn out well in this position.
} 10. g4 f5 11. gxf5 gxf5 12. exf5 Bxf5 {The pawn exchanges have left White
with two isolated weak pawns but have improved the scope of his pieces.
Black's position doesn't look unattractive, but White quickly highlights its
weaknesses.} 13. Ng3 Bg6 14. Qd2 $1 {A move with several points. It produces a
queen and bishop battery with the obvious intention of invading eventually at
h6. In addition, the queen is now able to operate on the second rank and
switch to the kingside if need be. Also, the e1-a5 diagonal is protected in
the event of White moving the queen's knight. White has his eye on the hole at
e6 (i.e. Nc3-e4-g5) and he wants to make the best of the black weakness on the
h3-c8 diagonal by Bh3 - hence he does not want to protect his f-pawn with his
king's bishop, which is why he now offers it. It cannot be taken safely: 14...
Rxf3 15 Be2 Rf8 (there is no better square) 16 h4 with formidable attacking
possibilities. Black wisely refrains from taking it and develops instead.}
14... Nd7 15. Nce4 Ndf6 16. Ng5 Nc7 {Guarding e6 and threatening the d-pawn.}
17. Bh3 {So that if Black captures the d-pawn he loses material to Be6+.} 17...
Nh5 {Hoping to relieve his position through exchanges.} 18. Nxh5 Bxh5 19. Rg1
$1 {Now, if 19...Bxf3? there would follow 20 Nxf3 Rxf3 21 Rxg7+ Kxg7 (if 21...
Kh8 22 Bg5 or just 22 Rg3 keeping the piece) 22 Qg2+ Kh8 23 Qxf3 and White has
an extra piece as well as his attack.} 19... Bg6 {
Keeping the g-file closed for a while.} 20. Rg3 {
Cool play to take the sting from ...Qh4+ once the knight moves.} 20... Qe7 {
It is difficult to see how Black can improve his position.} 21. Rc1 {
Quietly taking control of another line. It contains a sharp tactical idea too.}
21... e4 $2 {A blunder in a bad position. This just makes White's task simpler.
} 22. f4 Bf5 23. Bxf5 Rxf5 24. Rxc7 $1 {Of course.} 24... Qxc7 25. Ne6 Qc4 {
There is no defence.} 26. Rxg7+ Kh8 27. Rc7 Qxd5 28. Qc3+ 1-0

