1. e4 e6 2. d4 b6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Bd3 Bb7 5. Qe2 d5 6. e5 Nfd7 7. Bf4 c5 8. c3 a5
9. O-O Ba6 10. c4 Nc6 11. cxd5 Bxd3 12. Qxd3 Nb4 13. Qd2 Nxd5 14. Nc3 Nxf4 15.
Qxf4 cxd4 16. Ng5 Nxe5 17. Qxe5 dxc3 18. Rfd1 Qc8 19. Qb5+ Ke7 20. Rac1 Ra7 21.
Rxc3 Rd7 {Diagram #} 22. Rcd3 {It's infuriating to let the opponent escape
with a draw from a position like this - but how does White win?} ({The obvious
} 22. Rxd7+ Qxd7 23. Qxd7+ Kxd7 24. Nxf7 Rg8 25. Ne5+ {
is pleasant for White, but not decisive.}) ({Fritz likes my idea of} 22. Rdc1 {
and says White is winning after} 22... Qb7 ({not} 22... Rd5 $4 23. Qxd5 Qxc3
24. Qb7+) 23. Rc6 $18) ({But Fritz finds a crushing move:} 22. Re1 $3 Qb7 (
22... Rc7 23. Qf5 $1) 23. Rf3 $1 Kd8 {Nothing better} (23... Ke8 24. Rd1) 24.
Rxf7 Bc5 (24... Rxf7 25. Nxe6+ Ke7 (25... Kc8 26. Qe8# $1) 26. Nxf8+ {mating})
25. Nxe6+ Kc8 26. Nxc5 bxc5 27. Qxc5+ {etc}) 22... Rc7 $4 {
Throwing the game away again.} (22... Rxd3 {was the only move.}) 23. Rf3 $2 {
Eventually wins the queen but not the game.} (23. Qxb6) (23. Ne4 {
are both +- (Fritz)}) 23... f6 24. Nxe6 Rc1 25. Qd5 Rxd1+ 26. Qxd1 Kxe6 27.
Re3+ Kf7 28. Qd5+ Kg6 29. Rg3+ Kh6 30. Rh3+ Qxh3 31. gxh3 Bc5 32. a3 Re8 33.
Qd2+ Kg6 34. b4 axb4 35. axb4 Bf8 36. Qd4 Re6 37. Kg2 Be7 38. Qg4+ Kf7 39. Qc4
f5 40. h4 h5 41. Kf3 Kf6 42. Qd4+ Kf7 43. Qc4 1/2-1/2

