{[Notes from "Development of a Grandmaster"]} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4.
b4 Bxb4 5. c3 Be7 6. d4 d6 ({Theory recommends} 6... Na5 7. Nxe5 Nxc4 8. Nxc4
d5 9. exd5 Qxd5 10. Ne3 {with a slight edge for White.}) 7. Qb3 Na5 8. Bxf7+
Kf8 9. Qa4 Kxf7 10. Qxa5 b6 11. Qb5 Bd7 12. Qe2 Bf6 13. O-O Ne7 14. dxe5 dxe5 {
Diagram #} 15. Nxe5+ {An over-optimistic move, although perhaps the one most
in the spirit of the opening.} (15. Bg5 {would have given White a safe edge.})
15... Bxe5 16. Qh5+ Ng6 17. f4 Bf6 18. f5 Bc6 19. fxg6+ hxg6 20. Qe2 {
Diagram # This position was not too promising. My first wave of attack has
been beaten off and I have to spend time bringing up reinforcements. Black was
far too obliging in giving me this time.} 20... Qd7 ({He should have replied} 
20... Qd6 21. e5 Rae8 22. Bf4 (22. Qc4+ Bd5) 22... Qc5+ {winning.}) ({
White must play} 20... Qd6 21. Bf4 Qc5+ 22. Be3 {
but 22...Bb5 and 22...Qb5 both look good for Black.}) 21. Bg5 (21. a4 {
(to stop ...Bb5)} 21... Rae8 {is hideous for White.}) 21... Rae8 $2 ({
Very strong, indeed decisive, at this stage would have been} 21... Bb5 $1 22.
c4 Qd4+) 22. Nd2 Re5 $2 ({Better was} 22... Rh5 {
although by this stage White no longer stands worse.}) 23. Nf3 Re7 24. Rad1 Qe8
25. Qc4+ Kf8 26. e5 $1 {My attack has grown to enormous proportions very
quickly as Black has never dealt with his exposed king position. A pleasing
queen sacrifice concludes the game.} 26... Bb5 27. exf6 Bxc4 28. fxe7+ Kf7 ({If
} 28... Kg8 29. Rd8 {
wins simply, but the move played does not prolong Black's resistance.}) 29.
Ne5+ Ke6 30. Nxc4 Qb5 31. Rfe1+ 1-0

